{
  "version": 1,
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "c_t50001",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Packages and secrets",
        "",
        "Install an npm package from the notebook and use it on the server and in the page, then keep a secret in `.env`, out of the notebook and out of every output.",
        "",
        "You will install a package from a failing import, import it in both kinds of cells, add a secret, read it in a server cell and watch it being hidden. About 8 minutes.",
        "",
        "**Before you start.** Installing a package needs the network, once. This lesson adds the small package `nanoid` to the workspace (a `package.json`, `node_modules/` and a lockfile appear beside your notebooks), and a line `TUTORIAL_TOKEN=…` to the workspace's `.env` file. The last section says how to remove both. Offline, the install fails with the package manager's message, and the secrets part still works.",
        "",
        "Run the cells one by one this time, with **Shift+Enter**: the first code cell fails until the package is installed."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50002",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 1. A package from a failing import",
        "",
        "The next cell imports `nanoid`, which is not installed yet. Run it: it stops before running anything, says the package is missing and offers **Install**.",
        "",
        "Click **Install nanoid**. nimbo installs it with the workspace's package manager: the one its lockfile names, else pnpm when it is installed, else npm. From a terminal, `nimbo add nanoid` does the same. When the cell says **Installed: run the cell again**, do that: it answers a random id."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50003",
      "type": "server",
      "source": [
        "import { nanoid } from \"nanoid\";",
        "",
        "nanoid(10)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50004",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Packages belong to the workspace, not to one notebook: every notebook in this folder can now import `nanoid`. The **Environment** view (the box icon on the left) lists them under **Packages**, with **Add** and **Remove**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50005",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 2. The same package in the page",
        "",
        "A client cell imports packages the same way. nimbo bundles the package from your workspace's `node_modules` and serves it to the page; nothing is fetched from a CDN."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50006",
      "type": "client",
      "source": [
        "import { customAlphabet } from \"nanoid\";",
        "",
        "const ticket = customAlphabet(\"ABCDEFGHJKLMNPQRSTUVWXYZ23456789\", 6);",
        "const code = ticket();",
        "display.div().textContent = `Your ticket: ${code}`;",
        "code"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50007",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 3. Add a secret",
        "",
        "Secrets live in the file `.env` at the root of the workspace, never in the notebook. Add one, with a made-up value:",
        "",
        "1. Open the **Environment** view (the box icon on the left).",
        "2. Under **Secrets**, click **Add**.",
        "3. Name: `TUTORIAL_TOKEN`. Value: `tutorial-token-not-real-1234`. Save.",
        "",
        "From a terminal, `nimbo secrets set --name TUTORIAL_TOKEN` does the same: it asks for the value, so the value never lands in your shell's history."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50008",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 4. Read it on the server",
        "",
        "`env.NAME` reads a secret in a server cell; `env.has(\"NAME\")` says whether it is set. `.env` is read again before every run, so the new secret is there without a restart."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50009",
      "type": "server",
      "source": [
        "env.has(\"TUTORIAL_TOKEN\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50010",
      "type": "server",
      "source": [
        "const token = env.has(\"TUTORIAL_TOKEN\") ? env.TUTORIAL_TOKEN : \"not set yet\";",
        "console.log(\"the token is\", token);",
        "({ length: token.length, token })"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50011",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Run both cells after adding the secret. The cell uses the real value (its length is right), but its output shows `***` in its place, in the printed line and in the result alike: nimbo hides the values of `.env` in everything a cell shows. Even the grey hint after the first line says `'***'`.",
        "",
        "Only values of 8 characters or more are hidden, so that short values such as a port number do not mask ordinary output."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50012",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 5. Secrets stay off the page",
        "",
        "A client cell sees only the names that start with `PUBLIC_` or end with `_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`: keys meant for browsers. Any other name is refused. The next cell fails **on purpose**; its error says what to do instead: read the secret in a server cell and export a function that uses it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50013",
      "type": "client",
      "source": [
        "env.TUTORIAL_TOKEN"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t50014",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Try it, then clean up",
        "",
        "1. Write a server cell that prints `env.TUTORIAL_TOKEN.toUpperCase()`. Is the uppercase version hidden too? (It is not a value from `.env`, so it is not.) What does that tell you about transforming secrets before you print them?",
        "2. Export a function from a server cell that returns `token.length`, and call it from a client cell.",
        "",
        "To undo this lesson: remove the `TUTORIAL_TOKEN` line from `.env` (any text editor), and remove `nanoid` with **Remove** in the Environment view, or `nimbo packages remove --names nanoid` in a terminal. If nothing else uses them, you can delete `package.json`, the lockfile and `node_modules/` too.",
        "",
        "Next: `06-routes`, a cell that answers HTTP requests."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
